          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "For newly diagnosed high blood pressure, first-line treatment often involves both lifestyle measures and medication, depending on the stage of hypertension and other health conditions.\n\nAccording to Major cardiology society consensus guidance (educational summary), drug treatment is recommended alongside lifestyle measures for:\n*   **Stage 2 hypertension:** This usually begins with two medications because a single agent is often insufficient.\n*   **Stage 1 hypertension** with established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk at or above 10 percent.\n\nIf an individual has **Stage 1 hypertension without these features**, lifestyle measures are recommended first. Drug treatment is considered after three to six months if blood pressure remains high.\n\nThe four first-line drug classes for most adults, in the absence of other specific medical reasons, include (from \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\"):\n*   Thiazide-type diuretics\n*   Angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\n*   Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)\n*   Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers\n\nIt's important to note that an ACE inhibitor and an ARB should not be combined, as this combination can cause harm without providing additional benefit.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
